  "inventory_reason": "Three substantive rules in paragraphs 1-3 are inventoried. The preamble prescribes those rules and defines the covered transfers; it is retained verbatim in the original representation, not treated as an independent ongoing command. Paragraph 4 is the common activation provision, quoted and attached to each rule, not an independent duty with a separate first-end history. No title, signature, page furniture, agriculture rule or contents entry is an operative unit.",
  "chronology_reasoning": "Partial producer finding. The complete official EO9837 text contains three ongoing transfer/computation/construction rules, effective as of November 13, 1946, with no own-term termination. Full EO12292 sections 10(c) and 11 establish express revocation as of February 15, 1981, not the February 23 signature. Named subject and Foreign Service Reserve searches locate genuinely related personnel and leave sources. Distinct reinstatement, administrative delegations, chief-of-mission leave, and later employee-donation rules are not mechanically treated as termination events. The held 1951-Act references and 1963 transfer/accrual rules make statutory displacement and leave savings a material unresolved issue. Full EO12293 adds a specific 1980 transition lead. Those operative statutes were not found among held extracts or the twelve retained PDFs. First cessation dates, earliest whole-order end and final savings treatment remain null; the established 1981 express revocation is retained only as a later event. Provisional status remains no. No image reading, human certification, universal statutory exhaustion or canonical publication is claimed.",
  "savings_reasoning": "EO9837 contains no sunset or explicit saving; its November 13, 1946 date activates rather than ends the rules. EO12292 contains no target-specific saving and expressly revokes the order effective February 15, 1981. EO12293 section 5 addresses pay and benefits pending conversion under sections 2101 and 2107 of the 1980 Act, creating an actual transitional-statute lead; it does not itself establish survival or revival of these leave-transfer rules. EO10903 section 5(b), EO11228 section 5, EO10893 section 306 and EO11034 section 11 preserve actions within their respective functions; these are not blanket findings that EO9837 survives the 1981 revocation. Accrued leave balances and completed transfers are not treated as erased. Earlier statutory repeal/savings and the treatment of accrued/transferred leave remain unresolved, so no final savings state is asserted.",
